22:48:27 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It’s a completely fair question—and a very human one. When literally every authority figure in your life repeats the same warning, it’s hard to believe they could all be mistaken. But this particular myth is a perfect storm of how medical folklore spreads and sticks, even among well-meaning doctors. Here’s why “everyone” could be wrong about this, generation after generation. ### 1. It Sounds Like It *Should* Be True The logic is seductive: you’re forcefully grinding bones together or making a harsh, unnatural noise in a joint. It *feels* like you’re doing something mechanically destructive. The sound is sharp and vaguely alarming. So the leap from “crack” to “wearing out your joints” feels intuitive—much more intuitive than “a harmless gas bubble collapsing.” Our brains are wired to accept cause-and-effect stories that fit that intuitive pattern, even without proof. ### 2. Doctors Passed Down “Grandma’s Wisdom” Without Checking Family doctors from your mom’s era (and before) often blended clinical training with community wisdom. A doctor might have heard it from his own parents, or from a senior physician who treated it as common knowledge. For most of the 20th century, arthritis was poorly understood, and there was no robust study to contradict the claim. So the advice got passed down authoritatively, not from evidence, but from cultural habit. The Dr. Unger story is funny precisely because he had to do a decades-long experiment to disprove something doctors had been stating as fact for a century without any data at all. ### 3. The Myth Has a Historical Origin That Made Sense at the Time The idea gained scientific-sounding traction in the early 1900s. Some early rheumatologists believed that “popping” joints could be a mild form of trauma that might lead to degenerative changes. This was a hypothesis, not a proven fact, but it got printed in a few textbooks and popular health manuals. Even today, if no one actively unlearns it, that old, unchallenged statement keeps echoing. ### 4. Correlation Can Be Mistaken for Causation People often start cracking their knuckles as kids or teenagers. Arthritis generally shows up decades later, often in the same hands. If a grandparent had both a lifelong knuckle-cracking habit *and* arthritis at age 70, it’s natural for a family to connect the two: “See, Aunt Carol cracked her knuckles and her fingers got all knobby.” But the rigorous studies show that Aunt Carol’s arthritis came from the same genetic and aging factors that cause it in non-crackers. Her cracking just gave the family a convenient, observable villain. It’s a classic case of “with” being mistaken for “because of.” ### 5. Medical Knowledge Evolves, and Old Information Lingers If your mom’s doctor delivered that advice when you were a kid, it was likely before or around the time larger modern studies were being published (the key one in 1990, a bigger one in 2011). Practicing clinicians often don’t immediately update every piece of common-wisdom advice unless it’s in their specialty. An orthopedic surgeon or rheumatologist today would almost certainly tell you the myth is busted. A busy GP from 1995 might well have just repeated what they’d always heard. **So, were they all “wrong”?** Technically, yes. But it’s a wrongness born of love (your grandma genuinely wanted to protect your joints), passed down through cultural transmission, and endorsed by doctors who hadn’t yet caught up with the evidence. It isn’t that they were foolish—it’s that this particular piece of folk medicine had a strangely strong grip, and it took a guy cracking only one hand for sixty years to finally break it. You’re in the fortunate position of knowing the real science now, while still understanding why the people who raised you believed what they did. That’s a kind of wisdom in itself.
